  "notes": "Measured on the 737 MAX 8 from 9Y-CAL (left, level, 2026-02-18) fitted to shapes/B38M.json, checked on 9Y-ANT (right, 2025-11-20), 9Y-JAM (2025), 9Y-BAH (2024) and 9Y-TTO (2022). 'Caribbean' runs x 0.171 to 0.352 just over the windows, its capitals about 0.8 m tall and its lowercase 0.78 of them. The right side carries it over the same stretch. The belly title was read off photos from below (9Y-BAR, 9Y-JAM) and its run is estimated. The hummingbird is the airline's artwork. The fin's areas carry its major color fields (traced with photo_trace fields at half a meter) and the tail cone's orange and crimson; its many thin yellow, lime and sky-blue streaks stay the logo's. The art faces the same way on both sides. Small 'BOEING 737-8' lettering over the aft windows and a small decal under 9Y-JAM's cockpit in 2023 aren't drawn. The ATR 72-600s wear the scheme too, with their own title, registration and a ribbon hummingbird along the windows (`types`, AT76). The 737-800s weren't measured. No open photo shows one in this scheme. When it was unveiled in 2020 the twelve 737-800s were said unlikely to be repainted, and the newest open photo of one (9Y-JME, 2019) shows the 2007 scheme. So a 737-800 drawn in this scheme may be wrong.",
  "history": "New. Replaced the 2007 scheme (white with red-to-purple script 'Caribbean Airlines' titles and the first hummingbird on the fin). It was first shown on ATR 72-600 9Y-TTI in March 2020 and came with the 737 MAX 8s delivered from early 2022 (9Y-BAH in February 2022). Some 2007-scheme aircraft flew on after that (ATR 9Y-TTB in December 2022)."
